SR-type
	rrrr bbbb oox0 0o00
	(x == 0 || b[6,7] == 11)
	o=000: sll r, b
	o=001: sra r, b
	o=010: srl r, b
	o=011: xor r, b
	o=100: or r, b
	o=101: and r, b
	x=0,o=110: mul r, b
	x=0,o=111: mulh r, b
	x=1,o=110: div r, b
	x=1,o=111: jalr r, b
SI-type
	rrrr iiii oox0 0o00
	     3210
	(x == 1 && b[6,7] != 11)
	o=000: slli r, i
	o=001: srai r, i
	o=010: srli r, i
	o=011: xori r, i
	o=100: ori r, i
	o=101: andi r, i
LS-type
	rrrr bbbb iii1 0o00
	          210
	o=0: sw r, i(b)
	o=1: lw r, i(b)
B-type
	rrrr iiii iiii 1o00
	     7654 3210
	o=0: bnez r, i
	o=1: beqz r, i
R-type
	rrrr bbbb aaaa oo10
	o=00: add r, a, b
	o=01: sub r, a, b
	o=10: slt r, a, b
	o=11: sltu r, a, b
I-type
	rrrr iiii iiii io01
	     7654 3210 8
	o=0: addi r, i
	o=1: li r, i
J-type
	iirr iiii iiii ii11
	ba   7654 3210 98
	jal r, i

TODO: circuit needs to be updated to reflect li loading a 9 bit signed immediate instead of an unsigned one

NOTES:
a word is 16 bits and is the only addressable unit
currently, and unfortunately, program memory and data memory are seperate
both program and data memory are limited to 2^16 words each
both also are read in one cycle, which is somewhat absurd (no cache yet)
this necessitates and extremely slow clock or impossibly fast memory

pseudo ops:
nop -> sll x0, x0
mv %r, %a -> add %r, %a, x0
lli %r, $i -> li %r, $i[8..16]; slli %r, 8; addi %r, $i[0..8]
beq(%tmp) %a, %b, $i -> sub %tmp, %a, %b; beqz %tmp, $i
bne(%tmp) %a, %b, $i -> sub %tmp, %a, %b; bnez %tmp, $i
blt(%tmp) %a, %b, $i -> slt %tmp, %a, %b; bnez %tmp, $i
bge(%tmp) %a, %b, $i -> slt %tmp, %a, %b; beqz %tmp, $i
ble(%tmp) %a, %b, $i -> slt %tmp, %b, %a; beqz %tmp, $i
bgt(%tmp) %a, %b, $i -> slt %tmp, %b, %a; bnez %tmp, $i
bltu(%tmp) %a, %b, $i -> sltu %tmp, %a, %b; bnez %tmp, $i
bgeu(%tmp) %a, %b, $i -> sltu %tmp, %a, %b; beqz %tmp, $i
bleu(%tmp) %a, %b, $i -> sltu %tmp, %b, %a; beqz %tmp, $i
bgtu(%tmp) %a, %b, $i -> sltu %tmp, %b, %a; bnez %tmp, $i
call $i -> jal x1, $i
call %r -> jalr x1, %r
ret -> jalr x0, x1

There are 15 registers and a zero register
Conventions:
0	zero	always zero	-
1	ra	return address	caller saved
2..=5	t0..=t3	temporary	caller saved
6..=9	a0..=a3	args/return	caller saved
10..=14	s0..=s4 saved		callee saved
15	sp	stack pointer	-

all IO is memory mapped;
for general serial/parallel io ports:
writes to IO mapped words are non-blocking: the receiver must buffer it
writes set then clear the word's new data bit: the receiver may buffer this too
when the new data bit is cleared, the value of the word is undefined
reads on IO mapped words block until the new data bit is set
reads buffer the incoming data when the new data bit is set
synchronizing requires the following cooperation from the connected device:
1. device A writes to word X which is connected to device B
2. device A begins a blocking read on word X
3. device B reads the value from word X and processes it
4. device B writes a dummy value to word X when it is ready to receive again
5. device A unblocks and reads the dummy value from word X
for output:
to the program, the memory is accessed like regular memory
the only difference is that some other device is reading it asynchronously
for input:
