interrupts:
NOTE: this section is work-in-progress and very much TODO :)
dmem[0] acts as a special register storing the interrupts mask
dmem[0] is initially 0 (all interrupts disabled), to allow the program a chance to set up the IVT before it is used
dmem[1] is the return address that an interrupt handler should use
dmem[9..=24] is the interrupt vector table; dmem[9] corresponds to dmem[1]'s low bit, dmem[10] is for dmem[1]'s next bit, and so on

ALU operation codes
0000: ADD
0001: SUB
0010: SLT
0011: SLTU
0100: DIV
1000: SLL
1001: SRA
1010: SRL
1011: XOR
1100: OR
1101: AND
1110: MUL
1111: MULH

Why is there...
...16-bit instructions instead of 32-bit instructions?
I can't compete with RISCV, so my ISA needs to be different, that difference being mine fitting all functionality in half the bits
...no beq/bne?
There was initially, but meant B-type instructions only had a 4-bit immediate which provides much less utility than an 8-bit immediate. Additionally, beq/bne can be implemented with sub followed by beqz/bnez.
...no method of addressing bytes?
This introduces complexity and there is not enough bits to accomidate new instructions to support this.
...no unsigned multiply/divide instructions?
I ran out of instruction bits, and this can be more easily done with shifts/adds/subtracts than signed multiplication/division. Also, you only need to work around this when working with very large numbers that are unequal when interpreted unsigned vs. 2's complement
...no remainder instruction?
I ran out of instruction bits, and this can be done with divide and subtract.
